{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>אֶת מִשְׁפָּטַי תַּעֲשׂוּ וְאֶת חֻקֹּתַי תִּשְׁמְרוּ.</b> Here he put the mishpatim before the chukim and afterward reversed the order and said ״וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם אֶת חֻקּוֹתַי וְאֶת מִשְׁפָּטַי״, because the first verse speaks of the reward of this world, as it says ״לָלֶכֶת בָּהֶם״, for in this world man is called one who walks, since he is transported from level to level, as it is written (Zechariah 3:7): ״וְנָתַתִּי לְךָ מַהְלְכִים בֵּין הָעוֹמְדִים הָאֵלֶּה״, for the angel is called one who stands, for he stands in one level, but man is called one who walks. And so in the world to come man too is called one who sits, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Berakhot 17a): the righteous sit and their crowns are on their heads, and sitting parallels standing, for he sits in rest and has already acquired all his perfection. And for the reward of this world he put the mishpatim, which are among the intelligible matters, before the chukim, whose reason is not revealed, to tell you that not only by the merit of the mishpatim will you merit to walk before HaShem in the lands of life — for your eyes see their benefit, for were it not for fear of Him, etc. — but even this: that through the keeping of the chukim, even though their reason is not revealed, you will nevertheless merit through them to walk before HaShem." "However, in the verse ״וָחַי בָּהֶם״, which Rashi explained as referring to the life of the world to come, he reversed the order — in the manner of 'not only… but even,' for in the keeping of the chukim it is obvious that you will merit the life of the world to come, for since you feel no benefit from them, it is obvious that all your deeds are for the sake of Heaven, in order to fulfill the commandments of your Creator. But even in the keeping of the mishpatim, for which you have already received your reward in this world and there is room to say that you fulfill them for your own pleasure, since the maintenance of the world depends upon them — nevertheless you will also merit through them the life of the world to come. And why? ״כִּי אֲנִי ה׳״ — for you fulfill them because I am HaShem who commanded you, in the manner that our Sages of blessed memory said (Sifra, Kedoshim, ch. 4:12): let no one say, 'it is impossible for me to eat pork and my soul loathes it,' but rather: it is possible, but the Holy One, blessed be He, decreed it upon me, etc. And therefore we find that he mentions the great recompense hidden for the righteous especially in connection with the chukim, as it is written (Psalms 119:33): ״הוֹרֵנִי דֶרֶךְ חֻקֶּיךָ וְאֶצְּרֶנָּה עֵקֶב״; ״נָטִיתִי לִבִּי לַעֲשׂוֹת חֻקֶּיךָ לְעוֹלָם עֵקֶב״ (Psalms 119:112). And the Yalkut concludes in parashat Ekev (Yalkut Shimoni, Devarim 951): ״מָה רַב טוּבְךָ אֲשֶׁר צָפַנְתָּ לִּירֵאֶיךָ״ — this is the reward of the light commandments. And whence does he know to say so? Rather, because the light commandments — people trample them with their heels (עֲקֵבֵיהֶם), and the Satan and the nations count against them, as it were, saying: what is this commandment to you? And this is found especially with the chukim, whose reason is not revealed and which are opposed to the people who count against them. Therefore it says (Psalms 31:20): ״מָה רַב טוּבְךָ אֲשֶׁר צָפַנְתָּ לִּירֵאֶיךָ פָּעַלְתָּ לַחוֹסִים בְּךָ נֶגֶד בְּנֵי אָדָם״, that is, in a matter opposed to a person's intellect — and that is the light commandments." "And he always uses the language of guarding (שְׁמִירָה) in connection with the chukim, for anything that has destroyers needs guarding all the more, as was explained above in parashat Bo (Exodus 12:25) on the verse ״וּשְׁמַרְתֶּם אֶת הָעֲבוֹדָה הַזֹּאת״, which is said concerning the wicked son who seeks to destroy the service. So too, this guarding mentioned regarding the chukim is against the people who count against the chukim."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The phrase ״אִלְמָלֵא מוֹרָאָהּ כוּ״ is a truncated citation of the well-known Talmudic idiom (cf. Eruvin 100b); rendered here exactly as the author's abbreviated form leaves it, cutting off mid-phrase.", 1 "The citation ״תו״כ קדשים קכח״ refers to Sifra on Parashat Kedoshim; the chapter number follows the author's referencing convention."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:28.608039668Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}